{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "217",
    "case_summary": "A 36-year-old woman with a 17-year history of migraine headaches was referred for management of a right-sided migraine characterized by a visual aura of gray-white spots followed by pain behind the right eye radiating to the right frontal, temporal, and suboccipital regions. She typically had 3 to 4 severe migraines per month lasting 3 to 7 days, with relief only from sumatriptan after trials of acetaminophen, ibuprofen, rizatriptan, methylprednisolone, prednisone, and dexamethasone failed. In January 2023, she developed a right orbital and medial cheek headache radiating to the right lateral jaw, ear, and down the right suboccipital region. Treatment with ketorolac, rizatriptan, and a 7-day dexamethasone taper failed to resolve her symptoms. She then received naratriptan, prochlorperazine, acetaminophen, ibuprofen, and diphenhydramine without improvement. Brain magnetic resonance imaging was unremarkable. Given refractory symptoms, she underwent an ultrasound-guided right sphenopalatine ganglion block with 4 mL of bupivacaine 0.25% and 1 mL of dexamethasone 4 mg/mL. This treatment also failed to resolve her symptoms.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Herpes simplex virus reactivation\nB. Primary migraine disorder was considered given her long history of migraines, but standard and interventional therapies\nC. HSV meningitis or encephalitis was a potential cause of headache, but the patient had no altered cognition, fever\nD. Underlying HSV\nE. Reactivation of dormant HSV triggered by the sphenopalatine ganglion block and corticosteroid use was considered given\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
